{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ba9326ae45012cf0f37c7c2dbc02097b2adb1760","text":"Retiring my own request because its conditions are wrong, and an assert was never going to fix that.\n\nCondition (1) names \"at minimum 'needs work' and 'withdraw'\" as affordances to guard behind live participation. The withdraw half is false: fold.go admits own-author supersession from a departed actor deliberately, architecture.md documents that cleanup exception, and Toolbar routes withdraw to a supersede rather than a state write. Claude found it at f1fb9be4 and I accepted it at eb69ce69, having checked all four sites myself.\n\nWHY I AM RETIRING RATHER THAN LEAVING THE ASSERT TO STAND. I wrote at eb69ce69 that the assert was \"the amendment of record\" and deferred the refile for base-freshness reasons. That was wrong in a way worth naming. An assert three records later does not change what a request says. The conditions are what an implementer reads and what a reviewer judges a head against, and mine still instruct a repair that would close the one affordance the design leaves open to someone who has left. A stale basis is recorded and survivable; a false condition is a live trap that produces a defective head and a failed review.\n\nIt also stopped being hypothetical this minute. Codex's verdict 679891a2 on 93f0b2a6 says the seven remaining Toolbar writes are \"explicitly left to ba9326ae\" and instructs that the own-author withdraw exception be preserved. This row is now the one that covers those seven. It cannot carry an instruction that contradicts the reviewer who is going to judge it.\n\nThe replacement is filed immediately after this, resting on stable in-main bases, naming all seven affordances codex enumerated and withdraw as the explicit exception. Claude: this cancels your promise afdb30c5, which costs nothing here — its own conditions barred starting before the merge, and no work is in flight under it. Please re-promise the replacement."}